      "content": "1.2"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Tripura is the second smallest state (by area) in northeast India and the third smallest in India overall. Its unique geography — surrounded by Bangladesh on three sides — creates what is sometimes called a 'peninsula' of India. The state lies between latitudes 22° 56' N to 24° 32' N and longitudes 91° 10' E to 92° 21' E.",
      "id": "wpih7f7"
    },
    {
      "id": "k2kzd55",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<img alt=\"https://images.openai.com/static-rsc-4/GNN4hfzuuw4sofxbex22_hwkho4xKNR2BfPGXZCGUzaDzGUqNu6n50dNlYeKn6SH_qx8iRezDU6GGTtePfRw4xn2h1I6iwmp7ZETFZujniBqK7bINyRnYDG9hGu-nQ-uT-auTSQljl4N0kQwvb6PTxtF38dmbYpa5TOTLEQfASG9XsKob0FAZONSRGBaCePQ?purpose=fullsize\" src=\"/books/Guide/Tripura_CONSTABLE/images/image_2wybj7z.jpeg\"/>Northern boundary: Bangladesh (Habiganj and Maulvibazar districts of Bangladesh)",
        "Southern boundary: Bangladesh (Feni, Chittagong, and Rangamati districts of Bangladesh)",
        "Western boundary: Bangladesh (Comilla and Brahmanbaria districts of Bangladesh)",
        "Eastern boundary (northeast): Assam (Cachar and Hailakandi districts)",
        "Eastern boundary (southeast): Mizoram (Lunglei and Mamit districts)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "<strong>NOTE: </strong>Tripura's three-sided encirclement by Bangladesh makes it one of the most geographically unique states in India. Because of this, goods from Tripura travel over 1,600 km to reach Kolkata through the 'Chicken's Neck' (Siliguri Corridor). However, the India-Bangladesh friendship has enabled use of Bangladesh territory, and the Akhaura-Agartala rail link (inaugurated 2023) now connects Agartala to Kolkata via Bangladesh in under 10 hours.",
      "id": "w5h9806"
    },
